1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:06,100
In Upper Arlington, Ohio, the least green part of town was 4.1 degrees hotter than the greenest part.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,200
So where should the city plant new trees?

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,910
Satellites photograph every street every few days. That's too much to look through.

4
00:00:15,010 --> 00:00:20,080
So we built a way to search satellite photos of anywhere on Earth, in plain English.

5
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:23,680
Pick a place and set a radius.

6
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:26,180
Type “parking lots”.

7
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:29,160
The best-matching squares light up on the map.

8
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:34,260
The same app adds two satellite measurements: how green, and how hot.

9
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:39,260
Greener squares are cooler, by about 1.7 degrees for every step in green.

10
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:44,260
“Plant first” marks hot squares with few trees.

11
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:46,530
Download the list.

12
00:00:46,630 --> 00:00:52,630
How does it work? One AI turns photos into numbers. Another turns words into numbers.

13
00:00:52,630 --> 00:00:56,630
We line the two up with one rotation,

14
00:00:56,630 --> 00:01:01,950
fitted on one million satellite photos captioned from OpenStreetMap.

15
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:06,050
We tested it on 50 regions held out from training.

16
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:11,050
About 1 in 3 top results is right. By chance, it's 1 in 10.

17
00:01:11,050 --> 00:01:13,780
For parking lots, it's 4 in 10.

18
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:19,900
A million photos helped only a little: 34% right, up from 30% with our first rotation.

19
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
It still misses a lot. Cemeteries and orchards score near chance.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,080
And in our own town, thin rivers look like roads: zero out of ten right.

21
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:34,400
Next: sharper photos, and an AI that can say “I don't know”.

22
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:39,400
Platonic Earth. Try it at the link below.
